7371094\n\nDOI: 10.1109/cleopr.2015.7375859\nAccession: 109308343\n\n\n##### Summary\n\nA roadmap for assembling optical metamolecule is not yet clear; to address this challenge, herein, we propose to use DNA origami to achieve custom arrangements of metallic nanoparticles for deterministic assembly of optical metamolecules.\n\n#### References\n\nLiu, W.; Li, L.; Yang, S.; Gao, J.; Wang, R. 2017: Self-Assembly of Heterogeneously Shaped Nanoparticles into Plasmonic Metamolecules on DNA Origami Chemistry 23(57): 14177-14181\n\n* * *\n\nHuang, Y.; Nguyen, M.-K.; Kuzyk, A. 2019: Assembly of Gold Nanorods into Chiral Plasmonic Metamolecules Using DNA Origami Templates Journal of Visualized Experiments: Jove 2019(145)\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nPiri Keşif Aracı (https://kesifaraci.com/index.jsp?fields=authors&modul=advantage-search&operator=or&p=1&searchword=Shelley+F.+J.+Wickham)\nciteturn126search2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 weeks ago; Publication Access Addresses Tam Metinler Summary & Keyword(s) Additional Information ... This research introduces “DIB‐BOT,” an open‐source solution combining a nanoinjector and a 3D printer. ... 10.1109/cleopr.2015.7375859 ... Oxford Journals Online ... We found that the DOT assembly yield (relative to the no cholesterol control) could be maximised by reducing the number of cholesterols from 6 to 1 (2 ± 0.2% to 100 ± 2%), optimising the separation between adjacent cholesterols (64 ± 26% to 78 ± 30%), decreasing spacer length (38 ± 20% to 95 ± 5%), and using protective ssDNA 10T overhangs (38 ± 20% to 87 ± 6%). ... Journal of The Royal Society Interface ... Biological Transport, Humans, Mechanotransduction, Cellular, Myosin Heavy Chains, Nanotechnology, Optical Imaging\nJ. Wickham】 , Steven D. Perrault , Sanghyun Yoo , Sung Ha Park , William M. Shih , Seungwoo Lee\n\nTerm(s) :\n\nFields, Waves and Electromagnetics, Photonics and Electrooptics\n\nDOI:\n\n10.1109/cleopr.2015.7375859\n\nYear :\n\n2015\n\nShelley F. J. Wickham\n\nView Publication Summary & Keyword(s) Additional Information\n\nSave Quote Analyze Citation Network\n\nSummary\nA roadmap for assembling optical metamolecule is not yet clear; to address this challenge, herein, we propose to use DNA origami to achieve custom arrangements of metallic nanoparticles for deterministic assembly of optical metamolecules.\n\nKeyword(s)\nDNA , Three-dimensional displays , Assembly , Plasmons , Nanoparticles , Geometry , Self-assembly , DNA , molecular biophysics , nanoparticles , nanophotonics , optical metamaterials , self-assembly , DNA origami scaffold , optical metamolecule , self-assembly , metallic nanoparticles\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nThe Kinematic Principle for Designing DNA Origami Mechanisms: Challenges and Opportunities (2015) | Hai-Jun Su | 1 Citations (https://scispace.com/papers/the-kinematic-principle-for-designing-dna-origami-mechanisms-53kfbd9epx)\nciteturn126search3 [wordlim: 200] Published: 11.3 years ago; Crawled: 3 months ago; Proceedings Article 10.1115/DETC2015-46833\n\nProceedings Article10.1115/DETC2015-46833\n\n# The Kinematic Principle for Designing DNA Origami Mechanisms: Challenges and Opportunities\n\nHai-Jun Su,Carlos E. Castro,Alexander E. Marras,Lifeng Zhou +3 more\n\n- 02 Aug 2015\n\nPDF\n\n1\n\nPodcast\n\nSave\n\nCite\n\nTL;DR: This paper aims to review the recent progress in applying DNA origami to design of kinematic mechanisms of nanometer scale, and presents a list of technical challenges such as design automation, computational modeling, and opportunities for mechanism and robotics community to apply the well developed kinematics theories and computational tools toDesign of nanorobots and nanomachines.\n\nread more\n\nAbstract: DNA origami nanotechnology is a recently developed self-assembly process for design and fabrication of complex 3D nanostructures using DNA as functional materials. This paper aims to review our recent progress in applying DNA origami to design of kinematic mechanisms of nanometer scale. These nanomechanisms, which we call DNA Origami Mechanisms (DOM), are made of relatively stiff bundles of double-stranded DNA (dsDNA) which function as rigid links, connected by highly compliant single-stranded DNA (ssDNA) strands which function as kinematic joints. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nInternal Error ()\nciteturn126view0 [wordlim: 200] Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://doi.org/10.1093/nar/gkv602\",\"lineno\":null}); \nFailed to fetch https://doi.org/10.1093/nar/gkv602: (500) Redirected to https://academic.oup.com/nar/article-lookup/doi/10.1093/nar/gkv602 which is not accessible via this tool. (non-retryable error)--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nMolecular ping-pong Game of Life on a two-dimensional DNA origami array - PMC (https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7398061/)\nciteturn126view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7398061/\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 365\nL0: 【0†Skip to main content】\nL1: \nL2: 【1†Image†cdn.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov】 Search\nL3: \nL4: 【2†Image†cdn.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov】 Log in 【3†Image†cdn.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov】 【4†Image†cdn.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov】\nL5: \nL6:   * 【5†Dashboard†www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov】\nL7:   * 【6†Publications†www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov】\nL8:   * 【7†Account settings†www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov】\nL9:   * [Button: Log out ]\nL10: \nL11: Search… [Input: Search NCBI] Search NCBI\nL12: \nL13: Primary site navigation\nL14: \nL15: 【8†Image: Close†cdn.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov】\nL16: \nL17: Search [Input: Search NCBI]\nL18: \nL19: Logged in as:\nL20:   * 【5†Dashboard†www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov】\nL21:   * 【6†Publications†www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov】\nL22:   * 【7†Account settings†www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov】\nL23: \nL24: [Button: Log in ]\nL25: \nL26: 【1†Image: PMC sear